## Stage 1, Project 1 — CartPole-v1

The simplest experiment. PPO with default Stable-Baselines3 hyperparameters, no parallelism, 25,000 timesteps on CPU.

The training curve went from `ep_rew_mean ≈ 20` at the start to `ep_rew_mean = 500` (the maximum) within about 10,000 timesteps. After that the curve was flat at 500 because the env caps episodes there — even a better policy couldn't earn more.

Three lessons came out of this:

**Lesson 1 — timestep ≠ episode.** A timestep is one action. An episode is one start-to-fall sequence. Early episodes are short (random policy falls over fast), so 25K timesteps might be 1000 short episodes early in training and 50 long episodes by the end. The `ep_rew_mean` rises as much because episodes get longer as because reward per step changes.

**Lesson 2 — more timesteps is not better past a saturation point.** Going from 25K to 1M timesteps didn't make the policy any better — it just kept training a policy that had already maxed out. In your experiment, you watched the policy *wobble*: `ep_rew_mean` dropped from 500 back to ~200 around 850K timesteps before recovering. That was the policy drifting through small parameter changes that happened to be locally worse. Real failure mode in RL.

**Lesson 3 — TensorBoard tells the story.** `policy_gradient_loss`, `value_loss`, `entropy_loss` all showed the expected shape: rapid early changes, then plateau. The `explained_variance` rose from ~0 to ~0.95, meaning the critic learned to predict returns well.

The eval result was `mean_reward = 500.00 ± 0.00` over 10 episodes. Perfect score, zero variance — the policy is fully deterministic and optimal on this environment.

