## What the final result actually looks like

When the policy works:

- Robot stands at the start
- Begins a trot — diagonal pairs of legs lift and step
- Forward velocity reaches ~0.28 m/s within 1 second (commanded 0.5 m/s — there's slip)
- Body height stays at 0.275 m ± 0.005 m
- The robot walks in a slow curve due to a small yaw bias (visible in your 60-second trajectory)
- After 60 seconds, the robot has walked roughly 17 meters in a wide curve

The discrepancy between commanded velocity (0.5 m/s) and achieved velocity (0.28 m/s) is normal for sim-to-sim transfer. The policy was trained in Isaac Gym with specific contact friction, motor strength, and timing parameters. MuJoCo's defaults differ, and the policy doesn't quite achieve full commanded velocity. To recover the lost velocity, you'd need to tune MuJoCo's contact parameters to match Isaac Gym's, or use more aggressive command values to compensate.

The yaw drift (robot walks in a curve) is a smaller mystery — possibly a small bias in the body-frame gravity projection, possibly an artifact of the policy expecting closed-loop yaw correction from an operator. We left it as an open issue.

